@foolip Our behavior for getters is based on how the JS engine calls us. We have two methods that they call us on. GetOwnProperty and GetOwnItem. The later is generally used when we see the bracket indexing [] and we get the underlying JS object that was passed in as the index (so we get a variable type and we can coerce it as necessary). We then call ToInt on that value without any additional coercion today.

The other variation where we have GetProperty which means a string was passed in we actually get an atomized property id. This we get the name from. Then we try to convert it to a number. If the conversion succeeds we use the index, if not we use the string. This is very high level I need to dig into the specifics.

I think our goal was to be as close to actual JavaScript semantics as possible (initially) and then where we needed to adapt that a bit to be more HTML like in our indexing behavior. JavaScript treats numeric and string indexes that look like numerics, as numerics AFAICT. Simple test case below. For non array objects all indices get converted to property names instead.

The issue with our collections is that due to the platform object dynamic namespace backing them (the DOM can change) and the fact that id's and names can conflict with ordinal indices, we aren't purely an array nor purely an object and thus our getters at some level have to disambiguate.

``` javascript
var arr = [];

arr[-2] = "foo";
arr[1] = "bar";
arr["1"] = "baz";

console.log(JSON.stringify(arr), arr[-2], arr["-2"], arr[1], arr["1"]);

var obj = {};
obj[-2] = "foo";
obj[1] = "bar";
obj["1"] = "baz";

console.log(JSON.stringify(obj), obj[-2], obj["-2"], obj[1], obj["1"]);
```

**Chrome, Edge, FireFox**

```
[null,"baz"] foo foo baz baz
(index):58 {"1":"baz","-2":"foo"} foo foo baz baz
```

